/// z-scores.
///
/// Each value less the mean, divided by the standard deviation, so that the result has mean zero
/// and standard deviation one: `(x - mean(x)) / sd(x)`, with the sample standard deviation
/// (divisor n − 1) that `sd` gives. Both are taken over the values present, each with the
/// reducers' warning for the rows with `NA` skipped (section 4.1 of the Language Reference), and
/// those rows stay `NA`. A column with no spread, one value or all values equal, has no z-scores:
/// every row is `NA` with the warning `0 / 0` gives (W0205), never an error, so a grouped summary
/// over small groups still prints. Like R's `scale` and Stata's `egen std()`.
///
/// x: The column to standardise.
/// returns: A `Column<Float>` with mean zero and standard deviation one.
/// details: The sample standard deviation with divisor n − 1, as R's `scale` and `sd` use; standardising by the population deviation is `center(x) / sqrt(mean(center(x)^2))`, not offered.
/// reference: R Core Team, the `scale` function of the base package.
/// reference: Stata, `egen` with the `std()` function.
/// see also: center, mean, sd
/// > let d = frame(x = [2, 4, 9])
/// > standardize(d["x"])
/// Column<Float> · 3 values
/// -0.83205
/// -0.27735
/// 1.1094
/// > frame(z = standardize(d["x"])).sd(z)
/// 1
fn standardize(x: Column<Float>) -> Column<Float> {
    let f = frame(value = x)
    (x - f.mean(value)) / f.sd(value)
}
